name: first program
intro: C is the language the machine under you is written in -- the kernel,
intro: busybox, the shell you type into. It is small: a few dozen keywords
intro: and a library. What makes it hard is that it trusts you completely.
intro:
intro: A C program is text. A compiler turns that text into a binary the
intro: CPU runs. Here that happens for you the moment you leave the editor:
intro: it compiles, runs against hidden cases, and shows what differed.
intro:
intro: Every program starts at `main`:
intro:
intro:     #include <stdio.h>
intro:
intro:     int main(void)
intro:     {
intro:         printf("hi\n");
intro:         return 0;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: `#include <stdio.h>` pulls in the declarations for standard input and
intro: output, which is where `printf` lives. `int main(void)` says main takes
intro: nothing and hands back an int. `\n` is a newline -- printf prints
intro: exactly what you give it, nothing more. Every statement ends in `;`.
intro:
intro: The int main returns is the exit status, the same number `$?` shows
intro: in the shell. 0 means it worked; anything else means it did not.
intro:
intro: A program has two places to print. Standard output (stdout) is for
intro: results; printf writes there. Standard error (stderr) is for
intro: complaints; `fprintf(stderr, "oops\n")` writes there. On screen they
intro: look the same, but the shell can split them: `2>/dev/null` hides
intro: stderr, `>/dev/null` hides stdout.
intro:
intro: You write the code in `vi`. Four keys get you through it: `i` starts
intro: typing, Escape stops, `:wq` then Enter saves and leaves, `:q!` leaves
intro: without saving. `h` `j` `k` `l` move (the arrow keys work too). That
intro: is all you need today.
---
q: hello.c -- the first program
spec: Print `hello, world` followed by a newline, and exit with status 0.
file: hello.c
need: - #include <stdio.h> first: printf lives there
need: - int main(void) { ... return 0; }   0 means it worked
need: - printf("text\n");  \n is the newline; ; ends each statement
start: #include <stdio.h>
start: 
start: int main(void)
start: {
start: 	/* print the text here */
start: 	return 0;
start: }
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	printf("hello, world\n");
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: hello
case: hello; echo $?
hint: printf with the text in double quotes, and \n at the end of it
---
q: fail.c -- say it failed
spec: Print `something went wrong` to standard error, not standard output,
spec: then exit with status 3.
spec:
spec: `fprintf(stderr, ...)` is printf aimed at standard error.
file: fail.c
need: - fprintf(stderr, "text\n");  prints to standard error
need: - printf alone goes to stdout; stderr is the other channel
need: - the exit status is what main returns: return 3; exits with 3
start: #include <stdio.h>
start: 
start: int main(void)
start: {
start: 	fprintf(stderr, "something went wrong\n");
start: 	/* exit with the right status */
start: 	return 0;
start: }
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	fprintf(stderr, "something went wrong\n");
prog: 	return 3;
prog: }
case: fail; echo $?
case: fail 2>/dev/null; echo $?
case: fail 2>&1 >/dev/null
hint: fprintf takes stderr first, then the same text printf would, and main returns the status
